WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.320
Film Courage: We’ve imagined that you’ve
received your share of screenplay pitches

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:06.160
or you’ve heard about others pitching.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:08.430
What are some of the wrong ways to pitch?

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:10.710
What are some of the wrong ways to reach out
to someone?

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:13.300
Shane Stanley, Filmmaker/Author/Instructor:
I think there is a right way and a wrong way

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:17.170
to present people your work (your screenplays)
to approach them.

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:24.200
I think if you break it down I think writers
need to be just as good if not better at writing

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:25.949
a synopsis or a treatment.

00:00:25.949 --> 00:00:30.119
I like getting those two page treatments where
you know if it’s five acts it’s a paragraph

00:00:30.119 --> 00:00:34.570
per act, a title, a longline, a brief synopsis
and then the breakdown.

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:39.860
I think if you can do that and basically summarize
what it is you’re trying to say I can tell

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:44.850
in two minutes if this is a story I want to
read and I don’t need to see a script.

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:50.920
But it’s really important you know the craft
of writing too because writing is a gift,

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:55.950
writing is a craft and a lot of people don’t
understand the importance of good storytelling

00:00:55.950 --> 00:01:00.039
and because they’ve spent a year and a half
writing a script it doesn’t mean it’s

00:01:00.039 --> 00:01:04.649
good and they have to respect that a lot of
people get hit with so many scripts that they

00:01:04.649 --> 00:01:06.659
take it personally if you don’t want to
read it.

00:01:06.659 --> 00:01:07.969
We all have lives, we’re busy.

00:01:07.969 --> 00:01:10.420
We have business lives, personal lives, social
lives.

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:17.090
We have all these things in our work life
and if somebody is kind, considerate and they

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:20.859
reach out I always try to say okay I’ll
look at a synopsis but only if it’s copywritten.

00:01:20.859 --> 00:01:24.109
I need to see the copyright, registration
receipt.

00:01:24.109 --> 00:01:28.009
I don’t take anything without that.

00:01:28.009 --> 00:01:33.560
Make sure you have your work copywritten and
be kind and considerate in realizing that

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:38.659
you’re not the only person who had a script
who needs read and reading a script does take

00:01:38.659 --> 00:01:43.020
people a couple of hours out of their day
and how many people do you know that you don’t

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:48.639
know who would take two hours out of a day
for a stranger to just read your script.

00:01:48.639 --> 00:01:49.979
I just think it’s about the presentation.

00:01:49.979 --> 00:01:56.279
It’s about knowing how to write a synopsis
or a treatment that can lure a reader or decision

00:01:56.279 --> 00:01:59.169
maker to want to know more about what you’re
doing is really important.

00:01:59.169 --> 00:02:01.709
Film Courage: How often do you receive cold
pitches?

00:02:01.709 --> 00:02:05.749
Shane: You know it’s not that often.

00:02:05.749 --> 00:02:09.899
I get a lot of inquiries which I really appreciate.

00:02:09.899 --> 00:02:11.299
I don’t mind people asking.

00:02:11.299 --> 00:02:12.439
I’m an open book.

00:02:12.439 --> 00:02:15.069
Anybody can email me (info (at) ShaneStanley(dot)
net).

00:02:15.069 --> 00:02:21.190
I don’t care…it’s how it’s done.

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:26.459
For me when I just get an email that’s just
shot and I know it’s shot I don’t even

00:02:26.459 --> 00:02:27.459
look at it.

00:02:27.459 --> 00:02:32.441
I just write back and say I don’t accept
unsolicited material and I cc [carbon copy]

00:02:32.441 --> 00:02:33.469
my lawyer.

00:02:33.469 --> 00:02:36.290
I cc my manager and I write them back.

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:38.489
And I say hey you know you sent this to me
unsolicited.

00:02:38.489 --> 00:02:41.281
I am not interested in seeing this, this is
not how to do it.

00:02:41.281 --> 00:02:46.510
Appreciate it if you have somebody who reps
you if they want to reach out to my manager

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:49.930
or lawyer, that’s a different story and
I never hear back from them again and that’s

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:54.060
just the end of it who reach out genuinely
because I do so much work with the colleges

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:59.031
that I will that I will encounter a writer
three years ago and they will write and say

00:02:59.031 --> 00:03:04.689
Dear Shane, you gave a workshop at my in Florida
a few years ago.

00:03:04.689 --> 00:03:09.579
I finished a screenplay and really enjoyed
your talk…may I send it to you?

00:03:09.579 --> 00:03:13.280
I always write back and say I’m not going
to read your screenplay right now but if you

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:17.010
want to send me…copyright everything…I
go through…copyright everything.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:22.340
I need to see that it’s been registered/certified/copywritten
with the USCO [The United States Copyright

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:25.860
Office] I will gladly look at a treatment
and we can go from there and usually they

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:26.860
are pretty cool about it.

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:28.310
I don’t mind reading those.

00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:29.310
I don’t.

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:30.310
I’m not going to read scripts.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:34.060
I just don’t have the time and I know that’s
terribly heady to say but I just don’t.

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:35.290
I’m a slow reader.

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:39.519
Film Courage: Shane what are the top five
reasons you will reject a screenplay?

00:03:39.519 --> 00:03:44.060
Let’s say it’s well written, it’s formatted
but there is just something that’s not working

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:45.060
for you?

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:48.590
Shane: You know in rejecting a screenplay
the first thing writers should never take

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:50.209
it personally.

00:03:50.209 --> 00:03:51.219
Art is nothing but opinion.

00:03:51.219 --> 00:03:56.659
I mean think about how many great scripts
got made years after the fact, after they

00:03:56.659 --> 00:04:00.440
were turned down and rejected by so many other
filmmakers.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:06.590
It’s about first and foremost it’s about
the project connecting with the person you’re

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:08.790
sending it to.

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:13.639
Just because I made a movie about football
and had success with it doesn’t mean I want

00:04:13.639 --> 00:04:15.659
to make another movie about football.

00:04:15.659 --> 00:04:23.199
I also want to evolve as a filmmaker so what’s
important is that you never take it personally.

00:04:23.199 --> 00:04:26.690
I would hope that if you do read a script
and I think there is something there that

00:04:26.690 --> 00:04:29.710
I would at least write, I would always try
to make it a point to write the writer or

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:33.000
contact the writer and say Look I’ve got
to be honest with you you’re talented.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.599
I like what you’re doing.

00:04:34.599 --> 00:04:37.370
I know somebody who I think would appreciate
you, let me help you.

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:42.000
I have no problem with that and one of the
writers that I work with and one of my favorites

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:47.800
to work with is CJ Walley, he’s the guy
with Script Revolution out of the UK and our

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:53.270
relationship kicked off by reading one of
his scripts that was beautifully done.

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:54.430
I would never make the movie.

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:57.699
I couldn’t sell the movie but it didn’t
mean he can’t write.

00:04:57.699 --> 00:05:04.960
I read his words, I read his set-ups, I know
his character arcs and I read his theory on

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:11.460
breaking down scripts and doing proper treatments
and synopses and this guy had it all together

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:15.150
and for me it was like Hey dude, I love your
writing.

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:16.590
I have a project I’m going to do.

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:18.340
I want you to write it.

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:22.780
We were able to work together and we’ve
written several scripts together and that

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:28.660
came because I read a script that I would
never make but I knew the writer was good.

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:30.660
So it’s not always about the script.

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:33.940
I don’t think spec scripts get made much
anymore.

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:35.190
Filmmakers have an idea of what they want.

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:37.789
We have actors that they know what they want
to do.

00:05:37.789 --> 00:05:43.250
We have buyers and sales agents who know what
they can sell at the moment and we have an

00:05:43.250 --> 00:05:48.720
audience trend that right now doing a drama,
living in an apartment is not going to sell.

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:53.919
We’ve all been locked in because of 2020
for 6 months in our four walls.

00:05:53.919 --> 00:05:55.599
We want to be outside, we want to laugh.

00:05:55.599 --> 00:05:59.259
I don’t want to hear about your problems,
I don’t want to think about my problems.

00:05:59.259 --> 00:06:03.759
So if we’re selling scripts right now and
you’re trying to pitch it better be adventure,

00:06:03.759 --> 00:06:08.400
it better be funny, it better have romance,
it better get me out on the water, I want

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:12.430
it to get me out in the desert, I want to
go on travel.

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:18.069
These are the things that you constantly have
to think about and writers are very myopic

00:06:18.069 --> 00:06:19.620
and that’s okay.

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:25.151
We get in a box and it’s all about this
and you have to realize’s actors, there

00:06:25.151 --> 00:06:29.620
is money that has to be raised, there’s
directors that have to come on board, there’s

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:35.199
teams of people who have to capture the vision
to make this happen and after that is all

00:06:35.199 --> 00:06:37.020
said and done is can it be sold?

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:40.780
Are there those 7 trailer moments in this
script that we know we can sell?

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:44.659
Is there an output worldwide?

00:06:44.659 --> 00:06:48.400
Or is this just going to appeal to one place?

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:51.590
These are the things that a lot of writers
don’t think about - the business of the

00:06:51.590 --> 00:06:52.590
business.

00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:56.000
It’s not just whether your script is good,
so much of it is about where is it going to

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:57.000
sell?

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:58.180
You’re still selling a widget.

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:02.970
Are you selling a widget that only appeals
to one out of every 5,000 people?

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:05.139
Or are you selling a widget that everybody
kind of needs?

00:07:05.139 --> 00:07:07.449
That’s how you have to look at it when making
a script.

00:07:07.449 --> 00:07:10.570
Film Courage: And what are those 7 trailer
moments?

00:07:10.570 --> 00:07:13.990
Shane: I don’t think they are anything specific,
I think they’re the catch phrases.

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:17.129
They are those hero moments if you’re telling
an action story.

00:07:17.129 --> 00:07:24.039
Like take any two minute super trailer that
you see and you’ll see those 5 to 9 moments

00:07:24.039 --> 00:07:26.370
that you know are important in the film.

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:32.770
They are the tent poles of the story, they
are the catchphrases, they are that huge explosion

00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:39.979
or that great moment in the chase, it’s
the the love scene real quick, it’s the

00:07:39.979 --> 00:07:46.660
action running, it’s those things and they’re
not anything consistent but your story has

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:51.319
got to be able to tell the executives who
don’t read scripts and don’t know much.

00:07:51.319 --> 00:07:53.759
It’s got to tell them that I can sell this.

00:07:53.759 --> 00:07:56.969
That’s all it is, it’s ‘can I sell it?’

00:07:56.969 --> 00:08:00.979
Some of the relationships I have with sales
agents and buyers they don’t read the scripts,

00:08:00.979 --> 00:08:06.699
they want the synopsis, they want mock art,
they want to see a mock poster that me and

00:08:06.699 --> 00:08:11.180
a writer make in two hours on photoshop and
say here is your kind of idea for the image,

00:08:11.180 --> 00:08:14.319
here’s your logline, here’s your title,
and they will tell you right then and there

00:08:14.319 --> 00:08:15.660
“Yeah, I can sell it.”

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:19.620
That’s how these decisions are made.

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:24.330
Film Courage: Wow…so why the treatment?

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:27.110
Why not just come up with a great poster and
logline?

00:08:27.110 --> 00:08:30.729
Shane: Well I think that is it but you also
have to have a script to shoot and finance

00:08:30.729 --> 00:08:35.340
so ultimately you’re going to need the script
but I think that’s all part of the package

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:38.130
because so many people from the filmmakers
side.

00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:42.960
I don’t write as much but if I have a writer
that’s done a script I expect the synopsis

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:46.220
and the treatment to be as good if not better
than the script.

00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:49.860
It’s got to be because I know there is always
three scripts, the one you write, the one

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:52.630
you shoot, and the one you cut.

00:08:52.630 --> 00:08:58.600
The treatment is what you’re really selling
overall.

00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:01.511
Executives are not going to read the script.

00:09:01.511 --> 00:09:06.970
They just want a two-page synopsis and throw
me some mock-art so I know what I’m selling.

00:09:06.970 --> 00:09:10.250
And they’ll tell you tell me in five minutes
I can sell it or you’re going to find somebody

00:09:10.250 --> 00:09:11.250
else.

00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:12.840
I can’t do anything with this, it’s not
my wheelhouse.

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:13.840
Okay.

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:17.560
And then we make the decision do we believe
in this enough to roll the dice and struggle

00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:21.750
and pull teeth and try to get it somewhere
else or do we listen to people we trust and

00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:23.130
say this is not the one to do?

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:24.720
That’s our development process.

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:26.200
It’s very quick.

00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:28.730
We don’t go through the years of development
or months.

00:09:28.730 --> 00:09:35.200
It’s here’s the idea, I’ll pitch the
idea to my buyers if they like it I say okay

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:40.060
they like it let’s do the synopsis and then
I get them the synopsis sometimes with some

00:09:40.060 --> 00:09:45.650
mock art and then they’ll either say okay
the idea is good act two you can’t do this

00:09:45.650 --> 00:09:46.820
in act two.

00:09:46.820 --> 00:09:47.990
This I can’t sell.

00:09:47.990 --> 00:09:49.780
Tweak it this way.

00:09:49.780 --> 00:09:55.390
And then they’ll go Oh in Act 4, maybe in
Act 5 it’s happening too soon or can you

00:09:55.390 --> 00:09:57.320
bring the villain in sooner?

00:09:57.320 --> 00:10:00.380
Those are the kind of conversations we have
and then it’s just the table is set and

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:02.720
the writer can go do it.

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:05.460
The spec script is very hard to sell.

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:08.540
I think spec scripts are great calling if
somebody can write or not.

00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:15.370
I found a tremendous writer through the community
college during 2020 who I take a lot of pride

00:10:15.370 --> 00:10:23.370
in and not only got her a manager she got
a job like writing a script for hire for a

00:10:23.370 --> 00:10:27.970
very substantial production company and this
is one of my Zoom students from the community

00:10:27.970 --> 00:10:33.200
colleges that reached out to me (you were
asking the right way and wrong way) and she

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:37.310
reached out to me, she had take three or four
of my Zoom classes with a community college,

00:10:37.310 --> 00:10:43.450
lovely woman, very articulate, kind, no expectations
just I would really appreciate it, I would

00:10:43.450 --> 00:10:45.910
love to share my work with you.

00:10:45.910 --> 00:10:51.100
Here is where I want to go in my career and
she’s an older woman, her son is like my

00:10:51.100 --> 00:10:52.100
age.

00:10:52.100 --> 00:10:57.980
She’s been around and she has worked around
the industry but never really flourished as

00:10:57.980 --> 00:11:01.802
a writer and I finally said email me your
synopsis and I looked at it and I said Oh

00:11:01.802 --> 00:11:03.230
my gosh, I know somebody who is looking for
this.

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:05.590
I said I’m not going to read your script,
send it to me.

00:11:05.590 --> 00:11:08.950
I’ll send it to somebody who knows this
company and the guy read the script and called

00:11:08.950 --> 00:11:10.380
me up and goes “Who is this woman?

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:12.020
She’s really good.”

00:11:12.020 --> 00:11:17.270
And we got her an agent and three weeks later
she actually got a work for hire on a greenlit

00:11:17.270 --> 00:11:22.741
film but they greenlit on the concept and
they liked her enough to say you were a writer

00:11:22.741 --> 00:11:27.040
for this and she’s on her second polish
on it and they’re getting ready to go into

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:28.790
production I think in January.

00:11:28.790 --> 00:11:29.790
It’s greenlit.

00:11:29.790 --> 00:11:30.880
Film Courage: Wow.

00:11:30.880 --> 00:11:31.880
How common is that?

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:34.300
Shane: Not very.

00:11:34.300 --> 00:11:39.911
But I say that to inspire because there are
ways and again it came from being kind, it

00:11:39.911 --> 00:11:43.870
came from being kind, it came from being a
familiar face, it came from somebody who reached

00:11:43.870 --> 00:11:48.810
out with a tender heart and a gracious heart
to understand and respect my schedule and

00:11:48.810 --> 00:11:53.240
maybe I didn’t want to read it and understood
when I said I don’t want to read your script

00:11:53.240 --> 00:11:56.930
but let me look at the synopsis and then I
was like there is something here and I said

00:11:56.930 --> 00:12:00.960
I’m going to send the synopsis to somebody
I think would like this and if they do you

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:03.620
need to have a script ready and that’s how
it went.

00:12:03.620 --> 00:12:04.900
Film Courage: In synopsis king?

00:12:04.900 --> 00:12:11.960
Shane: In this day and age of instant information
and being able to get answers quickly I suggest

00:12:11.960 --> 00:12:14.920
that it’s definitely that is put to the
forefront.

00:12:14.920 --> 00:12:20.170
Going back to people’s time and we’ve
developed this instant gratification generation

00:12:20.170 --> 00:12:23.530
of getting it on the phone and I’ll get
you answer, let met ask Siri.

00:12:23.530 --> 00:12:27.390
I think a lot of us have put that into our
workflow.

00:12:27.390 --> 00:12:28.420
I know what my days are.

00:12:28.420 --> 00:12:33.420
I hit the ground, I am working by 5:00, 5:30
in the morning even when I am not making a

00:12:33.420 --> 00:12:36.450
movie and my wife is usually saying alright
honey it’s getting late.

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:39.130
This is when we’re not doing a movie.

00:12:39.130 --> 00:12:43.350
I am immersed in my work so take two hours
a day to read a script, it doesn’t fit.

00:12:43.350 --> 00:12:49.610
I don’t schedule read days so my feeling
if you want to write and you want to get seen

00:12:49.610 --> 00:12:56.930
by filmmakers who are busy I suggest you learn
how to build a treatment besides being a good

00:12:56.930 --> 00:13:01.570
screenwriter, learn how to do a treatment,
learn how to do a synopsis.

00:13:01.570 --> 00:13:06.700
There is a web platform based in the United
Kingdom called Script Revolution.

00:13:06.700 --> 00:13:10.399
It was created by CJ Walley.

00:13:10.399 --> 00:13:16.890
I find if you’re a writer I suggest you
go and check it out, it’s a free site.

00:13:16.890 --> 00:13:23.591
I think you will be blown away on the way
it breaks down how you write not only the

00:13:23.591 --> 00:13:28.380
screenplay but the treatment, the synopsis,
the act breakdowns.

00:13:28.380 --> 00:13:32.790
He has created something called Turn and Burn
which is a really cool theory and I think

00:13:32.790 --> 00:13:35.590
once you understand that as a writer your
treatments are going to flourish which are

00:13:35.590 --> 00:13:40.380
going to make people in a position of making
movies get more excited about what it is you’re

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:41.920
doing and what you’re trying to say.

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:47.090
I think the treatment and the synopsis is
so important.

00:13:47.090 --> 00:13:52.790
The script ultimately is but if I read a great
synopsis and a great treatment I will be more

00:13:52.790 --> 00:13:57.510
forgiving on the script knowing where ultimately
this could go whereas if I just get a mediocre

00:13:57.510 --> 00:14:03.230
script and it’s okay and done I don’t
see the vision that you caught me in a page

00:14:03.230 --> 00:14:06.400
or two so I am a big proponent for the treatment.

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:09.390
Film Courage: And sorry real quickly what
is the Turn and Burn?

00:14:09.390 --> 00:14:12.030
Shane: That’s a good question, that’s
for him to answer.

00:14:12.030 --> 00:14:18.000
If you go on the website, if you go onto Script
Revolution(dot)com there is a whole sub-chapter

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:24.740
about Turn and Burn and is his theory and
how he creates the synopsis and it’s a whole

00:14:24.740 --> 00:14:29.710
map of like 10 different sayings that he has
that I could never relay.

00:14:29.710 --> 00:14:35.820
I don’t write anymore but it all makes sense
when I have collaborated with him or other

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:46.061
writers that are part of his platform, it’s
really about developing the treatment, develop

00:14:46.061 --> 00:14:49.170
the treatment so the script process is easy.

00:14:49.170 --> 00:14:51.119
It’s like as I talk about in my book [WHAT
YOU DON’T LEARN IN FILM SCHOOL: A Complete

00:14:51.119 --> 00:14:53.750
Guide To (Independent) Filmmaking] the most
important thing about filmmaking is the pre-production

00:14:53.750 --> 00:14:54.920
time.

00:14:54.920 --> 00:15:00.430
I work myself hard during pre-production so
production is fun and easy, that’s how it’s

00:15:00.430 --> 00:15:01.490
supposed to be.

00:15:01.490 --> 00:15:06.250
You know pro football players will tell you
work Monday through Saturday and you get paid

00:15:06.250 --> 00:15:09.570
to play on Sunday, that’s how it should
be when you write, that’s how it should

00:15:09.570 --> 00:15:14.000
be when you make a movie and what they’ve
done at Script Revolution is they have basically

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:20.950
given you training camp, hell week and practice
in the treatment side so the writing of the

00:15:20.950 --> 00:15:27.480
script is actually fast, efficient and right
on point and there is no surprises and that’s

00:15:27.480 --> 00:15:32.550
what most filmmakers, development executives
get concerned about is writers going down

00:15:32.550 --> 00:15:35.630
rabbit holes they weren’t supposed to go
down, going off track.

00:15:35.630 --> 00:15:41.300
If your treatment is in order everything is
expected you are just filling up the pages

00:15:41.300 --> 00:15:42.370
with witty dialogue, with fun.

00:15:42.370 --> 00:15:48.380
To give you an example when working on this
film we developed we got a six-page treatment

00:15:48.380 --> 00:15:52.570
that’s the five acts, it’s the character
breakdown, who everybody is, what the point

00:15:52.570 --> 00:15:56.000
of the show is, what’s the logline, what’s
the synopsis.

00:15:56.000 --> 00:15:59.380
We’ve gotten four acts in seven days.

00:15:59.380 --> 00:16:03.450
There is not one surprise except happy surprises
because we all know where this is, there are

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:06.660
four of us that have all been involved in
doing the film that are from a decision-making

00:16:06.660 --> 00:16:07.660
capacity.

00:16:07.660 --> 00:16:12.560
Not one of us have been thrown a bump, a roadblock,
a speed bump.

00:16:12.560 --> 00:16:14.820
Nobody has been like Oh my gosh, what’s
going on?

00:16:14.820 --> 00:16:18.590
And it was because the way the treatment was
handled going in.

00:16:18.590 --> 00:16:19.950
It’s so paramount.

00:16:19.950 --> 00:16:22.970
Film Courage: And speaking of bumps, I don’t
know if you’re reading my mind, but I was

00:16:22.970 --> 00:16:24.420
just going to bring up Goosebumps.

00:16:24.420 --> 00:16:27.880
That’s so funny, it was the next thing I
was going to say was that author R.L. Stine

00:16:27.880 --> 00:16:32.190
I guess the creator of Goosebumps he talked
about how he knows the ending and he writes

00:16:32.190 --> 00:16:37.550
this treatment or whatever and then once he
knows the ending he can go back and he can

00:16:37.550 --> 00:16:42.550
fool the audience and it’s effortless writing
but it’s just getting that structure in

00:16:42.550 --> 00:16:43.550
place.

00:16:43.550 --> 00:16:48.360
Shane: And I know in the way that they do
these treatments they will often have 17 blank

00:16:48.360 --> 00:16:50.730
pages and start on page 17 and work backwards.

00:16:50.730 --> 00:16:52.590
It’s not uncommon.

00:16:52.590 --> 00:16:55.760
It’s interesting and there are different
ways for it to work for everybody but it’s

00:16:55.760 --> 00:17:00.200
just kind of basically a way to think about
it and I think once you capture that whether

00:17:00.200 --> 00:17:03.890
you are a writer or not you see what he’s
doing “Oh I get it!”

00:17:03.890 --> 00:17:08.100
And too many of us when I used to hack away
I would just sit there and come up with a

00:17:08.100 --> 00:17:11.100
title and write Fade In: and tell my story.

00:17:11.100 --> 00:17:13.669
Sometimes I got luck and sold a few scripts,
I did okay.

00:17:13.669 --> 00:17:17.370
But as I got older my mind wasn’t as fast,
I wasn’t as creative.

00:17:17.370 --> 00:17:21.709
I had kind of burned a lot of that creative
energy telling stories and becoming a filmmaker,

00:17:21.709 --> 00:17:23.230
editor, producer, director.

00:17:23.230 --> 00:17:29.950
I wasn’t just sitting for weeks at a time
writing so I learned later in life how important

00:17:29.950 --> 00:17:31.090
a treatment is.

00:17:31.090 --> 00:17:34.710
It’s so important to know what it is you’re
going to do and it’s great when you can

00:17:34.710 --> 00:17:39.110
surprise yourself as a writer but to know
where you’re going, to know the purpose

00:17:39.110 --> 00:17:43.789
of each scene, to know the arcs, the backstories
that aren’t going to be on the page of each

00:17:43.789 --> 00:17:47.289
character I think if you as a creator know
all that you’d be amazed all the stories

00:17:47.289 --> 00:17:48.289
you can tell.

00:17:48.289 --> 00:17:49.409
It’s just smooth sailing.

00:17:49.409 --> 00:17:52.519
Film Courage: Shane we’ve heard you say
“Send us the treatment.”

00:17:52.519 --> 00:17:56.610
We’ve heard on our side people say “Okay
I sent them a treatment, it was a 20-page

00:17:56.610 --> 00:17:58.510
treatment, is that okay?”

00:17:58.510 --> 00:17:59.570
Is that too long?

00:17:59.570 --> 00:18:06.320
Shane: I think in first pass I think again
you need to be considerate of the reader.

00:18:06.320 --> 00:18:10.919
When I say send a treatment I would like a
title, I’d like to know who it was written

00:18:10.919 --> 00:18:14.889
by, I’d love to see a logline, and then
I’d like to see a two-page synopsis.

00:18:14.889 --> 00:18:20.350
Give me a paragraph per act, that it, that’s
all I need.

00:18:20.350 --> 00:18:24.470
I could either say yeah I like this I want
to see a script or I could say you know what?

00:18:24.470 --> 00:18:30.909
If you have a more in-depth treatment breaking
down some things, back story on the characters,

00:18:30.909 --> 00:18:34.440
cast size, I’d love to see it.

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:38.700
Then I would expect that 10 to 12 to 20 page
treatment.

00:18:38.700 --> 00:18:40.559
It’s not that you shouldn’t have them.

00:18:40.559 --> 00:18:44.659
I feel when I say you need to be versed as
a writer in writing synopsises and treatments,

00:18:44.659 --> 00:18:47.399
those are two separate things that are equally
as important.

00:18:47.399 --> 00:18:49.320
I am only going to read a synopsis first.

00:18:49.320 --> 00:18:54.179
I just want to see a page or two on what you’re
doing and if that instills not quite enough

00:18:54.179 --> 00:18:58.480
interest to see the script but I’m intrigued
enough to say I want to know more that’s

00:18:58.480 --> 00:19:02.419
when that 20-page treatment or whatever it
is that you’ve done is important.

00:19:02.419 --> 00:19:07.009
Obviously not a 90-page treatment then just
send me the script but you know what I mean.

00:19:07.009 --> 00:19:08.850
I think the synopsis is king.

00:19:08.850 --> 00:19:14.690
It’s just let’s ignite that fire, spark
interest and if you get that have the treatment

00:19:14.690 --> 00:19:18.139
ready but hopefully the script is there and
tight and good.

00:19:18.139 --> 00:19:22.700
That was a great questions, it is because
some people send you like a paragraph and

00:19:22.700 --> 00:19:24.019
you’re like that’s it?

00:19:24.019 --> 00:19:29.110
I always say a synopsis should just be a paragraph
per…you know a treatment is a paragraph

00:19:29.110 --> 00:19:34.710
per act and then the breakdowns but a synopsis
should be about a page and a half, two pages

00:19:34.710 --> 00:19:39.210
and if you need more than that to tell the
story you’ve got to re-look at your story,

00:19:39.210 --> 00:19:42.790
it’s too confusing, it’s too long, it’s
too hodgepodgy, you’ve got to tighten it

00:19:42.790 --> 00:19:43.790
up.

00:19:43.790 --> 00:19:45.759
Question For The Viewers: Do you send out
treatments before you send out your screenplays?

